## A Record-Breaking Year for Recalls

2026 is on track to become the year with the highest number of food recalls in recorded history. With a total of 714 recalls already this year (July), it is clear that something needs to change. The recalls are classified as follows:

- Class I (Immediate threat): 181 recalls
- Class II (Potential threat): 438 recalls
- Class III (Minimal threat): 94 recalls

This indicates a substantial portion of recalls (over 25%) are due to immediate health risks. This should be a wake-up call to both producers and regulators.

## Top Offenders: A Pattern Emerges

Analyzing the data, Moonlight Packing Corporation tops the list of most recalled products, with an abysmal grade of F. With 11 recalls to their name, all of which are Class I recalls, it's clear this company is struggling with basic safety standards. Other repeat offenders include:

- Ghirardelli Chocolate Company (10 recalls, Grade F)
- Ambriola Co., Inc., The (8 recalls, Grade F)
- Everpress Juice (7 recalls, Grade F)

These brands need to invest in better quality and safety measures to prevent further incidents.

## Nature of the Threats

Salmonella accounts for 178 of this year's recalls, allergen contamination for 286, and foreign objects for 100. Most recently, Al Yaman Halawa Extra Pistachio was recalled due to Salmonella contamination, a Class I health risk. This type of contamination is both avoidable and unacceptable in today's food safety landscape.

## Consumers Beware

While we await stricter regulations, consumers should remain vigilant. Check FDA recall alerts regularly, and avoid the products listed in recent recalls, such as:

- White Cheddar Seasoning from Jonco Industries (Class I - Salmonella contamination)
- Adndale Magnesium Glycinate Gummies from Dkiru LLC (Class II - undeclared melatonin)
- Great Value Hawaiian Roll from United States Bakery (Class II - contamination risk)

## Moving Forward

The food industry must prioritize consumer safety over profit margins. Comprehensive testing, transparent sourcing, and stringent quality controls are non-negotiable. As consumers, we have the power to make our voices heard by holding these companies accountable and demanding better practices.
